ncome is only $4.2B \u2014 the cash tax step-up will hit FCF just as growth matures.\n4. Leverage: net debt $62.5B + $10.9B Apogee = ~$73B; if rates stay at 4.7%, interest expense grows >$3.2B, consuming 10% of adjusted EBIT.\n5. Apogee: $10.9B for a Phase-2-stage asset at 50% premium; base-rate success is ~30%; even if approved, peak-sales contributions are partly priced in.\n6. Competitive whack-a-mole: Lilly's oral IL-17, J&J Tremfya, BMS, Novartis all targeting the IL-23/JAK classes; Skyrizi's 24% growth decelerates as base doubles.\n7. Dividend + defensiveness crowd: ETF/passive ownership at 78%; any missed EPS guide triggers 3\u20135% de-rating.\n\n**Logic combat:** Long (execution, moat, valuation 18.7x vs 16% growth) = 7/10. Short (LOE 2033, leverage, Medicare) = 6/10. **Gap = 1 < 2 \u2192 conviction downgraded.** \n\n**Adversarial data:** No Hindenburg-style report exists on ABBV in my data [Unconfirmed]; activist scrutiny highlighted by research brief on single-therapy concentration.\n\n**Killed arguments & resurrection conditions:**\n- \"AbbVie always navigates cliffs.\" *Killed:* Humira peak and its replacement were both smaller; 2033 cliff is 1.5x larger. *Revives if:* company signs settlement agreements pushing biosimilars to \u22652034 or builds >$15B non-Skyrizi/Rinvoq revenue by 2032.\n- \"The P/E is 74x, so it is expensive.\" *Killed:* GAAP P/E distorts via amortization; adjusted forward P/E is 18.7x. *Revives if:* adjusted-to-cash conversion drops below 75%.\n- \"FCF yield 3.9% is cheap vs bonds.\" *Killed:* the yield does not compensate for LOE tail risk. *Revives if:* 10Y < 3.5%.\n\n---\n\n## SECTION 11 \u2014 DECISION JOURNAL ENTRY\n\n| Field | Entry |\n|---|---|\n| T0 | 2026-08-24, $264.96 |\n| T\u22121 | FY2025: Rev $61.16B, NI $4.226B (GAAP), OCF $19.03B, FCF $17.82B |\n| Tpf | Q2-2026: Rev $16.99B +10.2%, GAAP EPS $2.03, Adj EPS $3.65 (beat); guidance raised; Apogee announced |\n| Base IV | $206 (DCF) / $215\u2013225 (blended) |\n| Scenario